WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.800
I want you to imagine, just for a second, flipping a coin a hundred times, and you get

00:05.800 --> 00:07.620
tails every single time.

00:08.620 --> 00:11.580
Statistically, that's practically impossible.

00:11.860 --> 00:12.040
Right.

00:12.280 --> 00:17.400
But then, a panel of, like, the world's most prestigious scientists hands you this beautifully

00:17.400 --> 00:22.880
formatted, peer-reviewed paper, and it mathematically proves that your coin is completely fair.

00:23.900 --> 00:24.360
Oh, wow.

00:24.900 --> 00:25.200
Yeah.

00:25.660 --> 00:26.860
So you look at the coin, right?

00:26.860 --> 00:32.420
And you look at the math, and your brain just sort of, it just stalls out.

00:32.800 --> 00:38.080
Because that cognitive dissonance, that gap between what a mathematical metric tells us

00:38.080 --> 00:43.000
and what is actually happening in reality, that is the absolute foundation of our entire

00:43.000 --> 00:43.840
conversation today.

00:44.780 --> 00:45.260
It really is.

00:45.540 --> 00:50.980
It's such a phenomenal premise for a deep dive, honestly, because we rely on the scientific

00:50.980 --> 00:53.740
method to be, you know, our absolute bedrock of truth.

00:53.960 --> 00:54.680
Yeah, exactly.

00:54.680 --> 01:00.220
We just naturally assume that if the data is rigorous and the math is super complex, well,

01:00.320 --> 01:02.460
the conclusion must be solid, right?

01:02.600 --> 01:02.960
Of course.

01:03.900 --> 01:11.380
But the architecture of how we measure success has developed these very dangerous blind spots.

01:11.380 --> 01:17.360
And to help us build a detector for those blind spots, we have a truly fascinating stack of

01:17.360 --> 01:18.140
sources for you today.

01:18.380 --> 01:23.560
So these are a series of essays published in October 2026 by an independent researcher going

01:23.560 --> 01:24.880
by the name Flyxion.

01:25.520 --> 01:25.840
Right.

01:26.160 --> 01:31.660
And I gotta say, reading through this collection was a wild, almost destabilizing experience for

01:31.660 --> 01:31.820
me.

01:32.200 --> 01:33.700
I can completely imagine that.

01:33.920 --> 01:35.420
It shakes up a lot of assumptions.

01:35.420 --> 01:37.060
It really does.

01:37.680 --> 01:42.120
Because Phlymation meticulously dismantles the hidden statistical errors published in,

01:42.200 --> 01:44.700
like, some of the most elite scientific journals in the world.

01:44.800 --> 01:48.780
I mean, I found myself in this state of just enthusiastic disbelief.

01:49.340 --> 01:49.460
Yeah.

01:49.540 --> 01:51.500
And setting the stakes here is crucial.

01:52.120 --> 01:55.600
It's really vital to understand that the published scientists we're going to discuss today,

01:56.140 --> 01:56.980
they aren't lying.

01:57.260 --> 01:57.460
Right.

01:57.540 --> 01:58.860
No one is faking data.

01:58.860 --> 01:59.300
Exactly.

01:59.660 --> 02:02.580
They aren't fabricating data or trying to deceive anyone.

02:03.060 --> 02:06.880
The issue is much deeper and, frankly, much more interesting.

02:07.740 --> 02:12.860
Human intuition just fails spectacularly when it encounters the mathematics of complex systems.

02:13.120 --> 02:14.700
Because we want a simple story, right?

02:15.260 --> 02:17.680
We inherently want a simple linear narrative.

02:17.860 --> 02:21.420
We want to say, component A was added and it caused effect B.

02:21.620 --> 02:21.900
Right.

02:22.160 --> 02:25.880
But complex systems, they just do not cooperate with simple narratives.

02:25.880 --> 02:30.920
And the methods we use to force that simplicity, like our baselines, our visual charts, our

02:30.920 --> 02:35.040
metrics, they often end up completely distorting the underlying reality.

02:36.000 --> 02:40.120
So we're going to dig deeply into how these fundamental errors happen, why peer review

02:40.120 --> 02:42.600
misses them, and how you can spot them in your own life.

02:43.000 --> 02:45.280
Because, you know, this isn't just about academic papers.

02:45.760 --> 02:50.060
Whether you're reading a medical diagnosis, an AI product launch, or a quarterly financial

02:50.060 --> 02:51.780
report, these illusions are everywhere.

02:52.360 --> 02:54.000
They absolutely are everywhere.

02:54.000 --> 02:59.140
So I want to start by looking at how researchers try to prove why a new invention works.

02:59.580 --> 03:02.620
Let's say you've built a cutting-edge piece of software, and it's amazing.

03:03.320 --> 03:07.460
Now you have to write a paper proving which part of your software is doing the heavy lifting.

03:07.800 --> 03:10.480
The standard method for this is called an ablation study.

03:10.840 --> 03:14.560
You basically take your invention apart, piece by piece, to see what breaks.

03:14.760 --> 03:15.000
Right.

03:15.320 --> 03:15.680
Ablation.

03:15.680 --> 03:21.840
So we have an essay here from Flyxion titled, Increments Are Not Contributions, which basically

03:21.840 --> 03:24.140
takes a sledgehammer to how these studies are interpreted.

03:24.740 --> 03:26.380
Let's build up the mechanics first.

03:26.700 --> 03:29.300
How does a progressive ablation table actually work?

03:29.740 --> 03:33.820
Well, picture a team of engineers building a new autonomous robotic system.

03:34.420 --> 03:36.220
They don't start from scratch, right?

03:36.620 --> 03:41.500
They start with a baseline model, perhaps an older open-source framework, and they measure

03:41.500 --> 03:42.140
its performance.

03:42.140 --> 03:43.260
Okay, so that's step one.

03:44.100 --> 03:46.520
Then, they introduce their first new invention.

03:46.780 --> 03:51.360
Let's call it component A. They run the test again and measure the performance bump.

03:51.840 --> 03:53.460
That difference is logged in the table.

03:53.880 --> 03:54.480
Simple enough.

03:54.960 --> 03:59.940
Next, they keep component A running, and they add component B. They measure the next bump.

04:00.660 --> 04:05.740
Then they add component C. If you look at the resulting table in the published paper, you see this

04:05.740 --> 04:09.000
nice sequential chain. You read it row by row.

04:09.200 --> 04:11.700
From the baseline down to the fully realized system.

04:11.700 --> 04:17.560
Exactly. But the trap springs when a reader, or even the author themselves, looks at the

04:17.560 --> 04:23.060
difference between consecutive rows, what Flyxion terms the sequential increment, and declares

04:23.060 --> 04:28.400
that this specific number represents the inherent standalone value of that one component.

04:29.080 --> 04:35.260
Okay, so to make this concrete, Flyxion points to a 2026 robotics paper by Lee et al.

04:35.260 --> 04:37.640
that focused on active 3D mapping.

04:37.980 --> 04:38.840
That's a great example.

04:38.840 --> 04:44.200
Let me set the scene for you. Active 3D mapping is essentially giving a robot the ability to

04:44.200 --> 04:49.040
navigate and map an unknown environment. Like, imagine a drone flying through a collapsed

04:49.040 --> 04:49.440
building.

04:49.900 --> 04:50.000
Right.

04:50.500 --> 04:51.860
Very complex task.

04:52.280 --> 04:56.460
The researchers built a pipeline with three brand new software modules to make the robot

04:56.460 --> 05:03.200
smarter. They started with a baseline model that had a completion rate of 61.81%. Not bad,

05:03.200 --> 05:04.400
but not great. Sure.

05:04.400 --> 05:10.000
Then, they added their first new module, called MAG, Multimodal Anchor Generation, and the performance

05:10.000 --> 05:12.060
jumped by 4.40%.

05:12.060 --> 05:13.200
A pretty solid jump.

05:13.340 --> 05:13.500
Yeah.

05:14.100 --> 05:18.660
Next, they added the second module, EMC, which stands for Exploration Mode Clustering.

05:18.940 --> 05:21.660
That gave them another 1.39% boost.

05:21.660 --> 05:28.620
Finally, they added HMS, Hierarchical Mode Selection, which bumped the score another 0.85%.

05:28.620 --> 05:33.980
Right. So we have 4.40, then 1.39, then 0.85.

05:34.340 --> 05:40.020
Exactly. So the paper looks at that 4.40% jump from MAG, sees it's the biggest number in the

05:40.020 --> 05:44.560
sequence, and basically claims that MAG is the most critical contribution of the paper.

05:44.560 --> 05:47.120
And that's where the illusion happens.

05:47.680 --> 05:51.820
Because the authors use that specific initial increment to build their entire narrative.

05:52.340 --> 05:56.980
They argue that the primary bottleneck in previous robotics models was deterministic

05:56.980 --> 05:58.060
single-goal prediction.

05:58.380 --> 05:58.740
Okay.

05:59.040 --> 06:03.080
And because MAG replaces that single-goal approach with multiple generated anchors,

06:03.640 --> 06:09.900
it unlocked that massive 4.40% gain. It sounds like an airtight logical deduction, doesn't

06:09.900 --> 06:10.040
it?

06:10.080 --> 06:12.540
It totally does. If I read that, I'd believe it.

06:12.540 --> 06:18.260
But Flyxion demonstrates mathematically that the ablation table provided does not

06:18.260 --> 06:23.400
prove that claim at all. The table only measures the benefit of adding a component to the exact

06:23.400 --> 06:27.380
configuration that preceded it, in that specific arbitrary order.

06:27.720 --> 06:31.640
I was trying to wrap my head around this, and I keep coming back to a mechanical analogy.

06:32.120 --> 06:32.820
Tell me if this works.

06:32.920 --> 06:33.340
Let's hear it.

06:33.600 --> 06:38.620
Suppose I want to build a really fast car. I start with a heavy steel frame sitting on blocks.

06:38.880 --> 06:41.080
The speed is zero miles an hour. That's my baseline.

06:41.400 --> 06:41.860
Makes sense.

06:41.860 --> 06:47.440
First, I bolt in a massive, 1,000-horsepower engine. I turn the key. The engine roars,

06:47.580 --> 06:52.600
but the car is still on blocks, so the speed is still zero. My sequential increment for the

06:52.600 --> 06:53.800
engine is zero.

06:54.040 --> 06:55.360
Right. Zero speed gained.

06:55.760 --> 07:00.680
Next, I bolt on a set of four high-performance tires. Suddenly, the car grips the road and

07:00.680 --> 07:02.160
rockets to 200 miles an hour.

07:02.280 --> 07:03.360
Oh, I see where this is going.

07:03.360 --> 07:10.800
If I publish my sequential ablation table, the data clearly states that adding tires caused a 200-mile-an-hour

07:10.800 --> 07:16.060
increment, and the engine contributed absolutely nothing. The conclusion would be that engines are

07:16.060 --> 07:20.740
scientifically useless for speed, and tires are this miraculous standalone technology.

07:20.740 --> 07:25.580
That analogy perfectly isolates the mathematical concept of interaction effects.

07:25.780 --> 07:28.180
Right. Because the order changes everything.

07:28.640 --> 07:34.500
Exactly. If you have components that are strictly additive, meaning they operate in total isolation

07:34.500 --> 07:38.940
and don't affect one another, the order you test them in is completely irrelevant.

07:38.940 --> 07:41.420
But they rarely operate in isolation, right?

07:41.900 --> 07:48.060
Almost never. In modern machine learning pipelines, and certainly in robotics, components almost

07:48.060 --> 07:50.860
always interact. They're designed to synergize.

07:51.100 --> 07:51.300
Yeah.

07:51.440 --> 07:56.560
So Phylexion's critique highlights that by measuring only the sequential increment, the

07:56.560 --> 08:02.880
researchers are completely blind to how much of that 4.40% gain is the standalone brilliance of

08:02.880 --> 08:08.540
the MAG module versus how much of it is a powerful synergistic interaction with the clustering

08:08.540 --> 08:12.500
and selection modules that were just waiting in the wings to process its output.

08:12.980 --> 08:14.600
Because MAG was tested first.

08:14.960 --> 08:20.160
It got to claim all the initial synergy. I mean, if they had started the experiment by adding

08:20.160 --> 08:24.920
the clustering module, EMC, first, the numbers might have looked completely different.

08:25.300 --> 08:28.540
We fundamentally cannot know from the published data.

08:29.260 --> 08:34.980
That is the glaring hole in the methodology. Phylexion actually constructs three entirely different

08:34.980 --> 08:41.100
hypothetical realities, three different mathematical universes for how those three robotics components

08:41.100 --> 08:43.060
might be interacting behind the scenes.

08:43.560 --> 08:43.760
Okay.

08:44.220 --> 08:49.740
And strikingly, all three of these contradictory realities perfectly reproduce the exact same

08:49.740 --> 08:54.760
4.40, 1.39, and 0.85 increments published in the paper's table.

08:55.480 --> 09:01.000
Wait, let me make sure I follow. You can have three totally different explanations for how the

09:01.000 --> 09:05.100
software works, and they all produce the exact same ablation table?

09:05.560 --> 09:10.520
Precisely. In Phylexion's first hypothetical reality, the components are purely additive.

09:11.000 --> 09:14.940
They don't interact at all. MAG really is just a standalone powerhouse.

09:15.240 --> 09:16.860
So in that universe, the paper is right.

09:17.200 --> 09:22.640
Yes. But in the second reality, the clustering and selection modules are completely useless,

09:23.020 --> 09:25.620
inert code, unless MAG is present to feed them data.

09:26.000 --> 09:27.200
Okay, that changes things.

09:27.200 --> 09:32.040
And in the third reality, the selection module is actually the true genius of the system with a

09:32.040 --> 09:36.580
massive standalone effect. And MAG only looks impressive in the table because it happened to

09:36.580 --> 09:38.340
be added first and triggered a synergy.

09:39.020 --> 09:39.420
Wow.

09:39.820 --> 09:44.980
The published sequential table is mathematically incapable of distinguishing between these universes.

09:45.400 --> 09:48.220
I do see a potential defense from the researchers here, though. Like,

09:48.300 --> 09:51.980
sometimes the architecture of the software literally forbids you from changing the order.

09:52.180 --> 09:52.460
True.

09:52.460 --> 09:57.320
If I have a module that clusters data points, I can't test that module until I have another module

09:57.320 --> 10:01.200
that generates the data points to begin with, right? The software would just crash.

10:01.480 --> 10:06.280
Right. And Phylexion explicitly addresses that. It's called a precedence constraint.

10:06.980 --> 10:11.880
It is very common that component B is only defined in the presence of component A.

10:12.160 --> 10:13.160
So their hands were tied?

10:13.700 --> 10:18.220
If the clustering module fundamentally relies on the anchor generation module to function,

10:18.220 --> 10:24.880
then testing MAG first isn't just an arbitrary choice. It's the only technically valid sequence.

10:25.580 --> 10:26.800
Okay, so doesn't that vindicate them?

10:27.500 --> 10:32.320
No, because that physical limitation does not miraculously transform the sequential increment

10:32.320 --> 10:37.940
into a measurement of intrinsic value. It simply means the contribution of the clustering module

10:37.940 --> 10:43.280
is forever conditional on its prerequisite. You still cannot mathematically declare that MAG

10:43.280 --> 10:47.220
is inherently more valuable just because it sat at the front of the pipeline.

10:47.220 --> 10:52.780
I see. So if the standard ablation table is creating this optical illusion, how do we

10:52.780 --> 10:56.220
pierce through it? How do we fairly assign credit to the parts of our machine?

10:56.620 --> 11:00.340
Well, Phylexion suggests borrowing a tool from cooperative game theory called

11:00.340 --> 11:03.200
the Shapley value or Shapley allocation.

11:03.860 --> 11:05.040
The Shapley value.

11:05.500 --> 11:10.840
Yeah. Game theorists developed this to solve a very similar problem. Imagine a coalition of players

11:10.840 --> 11:14.580
works together to win a prize, but they all have different skills that synergize.

11:14.580 --> 11:17.020
How do you fairly divide the payout?

11:17.580 --> 11:18.440
Okay, that makes sense.

11:19.240 --> 11:23.740
The Shapley approach says you shouldn't just test one sequence of players joining the team.

11:24.060 --> 11:26.240
You must test every possible sequence.

11:26.580 --> 11:30.320
So if you have three software modules, you test them in every combination?

11:30.960 --> 11:35.140
A, then B, then C, B, then C, then A, C, then A, then B?

11:35.140 --> 11:41.180
Yes. For three components, there are six possible insertion orders. You measure the performance

11:41.180 --> 11:45.860
increment that component A provides in every single one of those six scenarios, and then

11:45.860 --> 11:47.380
you average those increments.

11:47.720 --> 11:49.400
That seems exhaustive.

11:49.880 --> 11:55.900
It is, but it mathematically forces the synergistic interaction effects to be distributed fairly

11:55.900 --> 12:01.740
among all the components that create the synergy. It stops you from arbitrarily handing all

12:01.740 --> 12:04.780
the credit to whichever component happened to be inserted first.

12:05.340 --> 12:10.140
That sounds incredibly rigorous. But Flyxion also drops this fascinating caveat about the

12:10.140 --> 12:15.160
Shapley value. It's a mathematical convention, like a way of being fair, but it still isn't

12:15.160 --> 12:18.180
uncovering the absolute laws of physics, is it?

12:18.600 --> 12:23.220
It is an elegant convention, but you're right. It is not a causal measurement of truth.

12:23.900 --> 12:29.020
The Shapley allocation still fundamentally depends on the arbitrary boundaries of how you decided

12:29.020 --> 12:31.960
to slice your software into components in the first place.

12:32.460 --> 12:33.100
That's a good point.

12:33.600 --> 12:38.060
More importantly, it depends entirely on the metric you choose to score the system.

12:38.720 --> 12:42.940
Flyxion highlights a stunning detail buried in the appendices of that same robotics paper.

12:43.600 --> 12:46.960
Oh, I love buried appendix details. What was it?

12:47.120 --> 12:52.340
If you evaluate the robot's success based on its completion percentage, like did it map the

12:52.340 --> 12:57.480
room or not, the MAG module accounts for 66% of the total improvement.

12:57.480 --> 12:59.000
Okay, 66%.

12:59.000 --> 13:05.200
But if you change the ruler and evaluate the robot based on its completion error measured

13:05.200 --> 13:09.480
in centimeters, MAG's share of the credit plummets to 52%.

13:09.480 --> 13:11.680
Wait, really? The data hasn't changed.

13:11.840 --> 13:12.800
The data hasn't changed.

13:12.920 --> 13:15.060
The robot ran the exact same paths.

13:15.500 --> 13:20.140
But merely changing the grading rubric drastically shifts the scientific credit.

13:20.500 --> 13:24.100
Which leads us perfectly into the second example Flightstick dissects in this arena.

13:24.100 --> 13:29.560
This essay is titled Same Backbone, Different Everything, and it looks at the world of large

13:29.560 --> 13:31.500
language models, or LLMs.

13:31.820 --> 13:32.620
Right, very topical.

13:32.880 --> 13:35.740
We are all familiar with prompting an AI to solve a problem.

13:36.180 --> 13:41.560
A paper published by Gong et al. introduced a new framework for automated heuristic design

13:41.560 --> 13:47.240
using an LLM. Basically, they built a complex software interface wrapped around the LLM to

13:47.240 --> 13:50.480
help it solve incredibly difficult mathematical optimization problems.

13:50.480 --> 13:52.840
And the results they published were staggering.

13:53.140 --> 13:53.940
Staggering is the word.

13:54.100 --> 13:59.000
Using a standard direct prompt to the AI, they achieved a success score of 0.571.

13:59.260 --> 14:05.140
But when they ran the AI through their new custom framework, the score skyrocketed to 0.945.

14:05.480 --> 14:06.360
That's almost perfect.

14:06.680 --> 14:07.480
Almost perfect.

14:07.700 --> 14:12.080
And the core argument of their paper was that because they used the exact same underlying AI

14:12.080 --> 14:17.300
model, the same backbone for both tests, that massive leap in performance must be attributed

14:17.300 --> 14:20.520
entirely to the brilliance of their new interface framework.

14:21.120 --> 14:24.660
And on the surface, that follows the most basic rule of the scientific method.

14:25.200 --> 14:30.080
You hold the independent variable constant, in this case, the LLM backbone, and you introduce

14:30.080 --> 14:30.640
your treatment.

14:30.860 --> 14:31.180
Right.

14:31.560 --> 14:34.240
The outcome changes, so the treatment caused the change.

14:34.340 --> 14:34.640
Exactly.

14:35.120 --> 14:40.300
But Flaggian meticulously unpacks what actually constitutes this quote-unquote treatment.

14:40.300 --> 14:45.480
When you look closely at the two test arms, you realize they didn't just add a polite

14:45.480 --> 14:46.020
new interface.

14:46.420 --> 14:49.540
They fundamentally altered the entire computational landscape.

14:50.320 --> 14:51.320
They didn't just change one thing.

14:51.660 --> 14:53.100
They changed the problem contract.

14:53.420 --> 14:56.200
They gave the model a pre-verified library of coding tools.

14:56.440 --> 15:00.180
And crucially, they changed how many times the AI was allowed to guess.

15:00.280 --> 15:01.160
That's the biggest factor.

15:01.500 --> 15:03.740
In the baseline test, the AI was asked for an answer.

15:04.000 --> 15:06.560
It generated one single program, and it was graded.

15:06.560 --> 15:11.180
But in the new framework, the AI generated 70 different candidate programs.

15:11.560 --> 15:11.840
Seventy?

15:12.020 --> 15:12.220
Yeah.

15:12.700 --> 15:18.460
It then ran all 70 programs on a separate practice test, a development set of 128 instances

15:18.460 --> 15:20.000
to see which ones worked best.

15:20.540 --> 15:26.680
Finally, at the actual test time, it ran a portfolio of the 10 best surviving programs

15:26.680 --> 15:29.180
in parallel and simply submitted the highest score.

15:29.560 --> 15:34.180
See, the phrase fixed backbone creates this illusion of a controlled experiment.

15:34.180 --> 15:39.700
But a fixed LLM backbone does not mean you have fixed the amount of computation, the amount

15:39.700 --> 15:42.140
of search, or the statistical variance.

15:42.380 --> 15:43.160
It's completely different.

15:43.460 --> 15:43.900
Completely.

15:44.680 --> 15:49.180
Flyxion builds a very elegant mechanistic toy model to isolate what happens when you

15:49.180 --> 15:50.860
introduce this kind of search budget.

15:51.500 --> 15:54.100
Imagine a generator that spits out random programs.

15:54.240 --> 15:54.400
Okay.

15:54.680 --> 15:58.960
Some programs happen to be good at solving instance A, while others happen to be good at instance

15:58.960 --> 15:59.240
B.

15:59.240 --> 16:03.900
Just the mechanical act of generating 70 programs, filtering out the worst ones, and running

16:03.900 --> 16:08.880
the top 10 simultaneously is going to yield a massive performance boost purely through

16:08.880 --> 16:09.940
statistical variance.

16:10.560 --> 16:12.960
You are simply taking vastly more shots on goal.

16:13.360 --> 16:13.720
Exactly.

16:14.060 --> 16:15.440
It is the equivalent of a lottery.

16:15.720 --> 16:19.760
I mean, if I buy one lottery ticket, my baseline odds of winning are effectively zero.

16:19.980 --> 16:20.100
Right.

16:20.100 --> 16:25.200
But if I buy 70 tickets, check them against a database of historical winning numbers to

16:25.200 --> 16:29.760
filter out bad patterns, and then submit my 10 most promising tickets to the real draw,

16:29.960 --> 16:31.780
my odds of winning skyrocket.

16:32.000 --> 16:32.160
Yeah.

16:32.360 --> 16:33.400
You probably do pretty well.

16:33.520 --> 16:37.680
But if I write a paper about this, it would be deeply misleading to attribute my newfound

16:37.680 --> 16:40.800
wealth to the specific brand of pen I use to fill out the tickets.

16:40.920 --> 16:41.280
Exactly.

16:41.440 --> 16:44.120
The success came from the fact that I bought 70 tickets.

16:44.320 --> 16:46.400
The portfolio effect is doing the heavy lifting.

16:46.400 --> 16:51.460
The portfolio effect is a massive, hidden confounder in modern AI research.

16:52.300 --> 16:57.900
Flyxion applies the Shapley allocation to this LLM toy model, and the results are incredibly

16:57.900 --> 16:58.400
revealing.

16:59.140 --> 16:59.740
What do they find?

17:00.540 --> 17:06.100
The paper claimed their specific tool library was a major innovation, but Flyxion shows

17:06.100 --> 17:10.900
that if he introduced the portfolio of 10 programs first, the apparent value of the

17:10.900 --> 17:12.320
tool library almost vanishes.

17:12.800 --> 17:13.060
Really?

17:13.520 --> 17:13.820
Why?

17:13.820 --> 17:18.520
Because the tool library's main purpose was to prevent the AI from making fatal coding

17:18.520 --> 17:18.840
errors.

17:19.360 --> 17:24.040
But running 10 parallel programs and taking the best one also guards against fatal coding

17:24.040 --> 17:28.360
errors, because it's highly unlikely all 10 programs will crash simultaneously.

17:28.840 --> 17:29.280
Oh, wow.

17:29.520 --> 17:29.700
Yeah.

17:30.000 --> 17:33.280
The portfolio effect functionally substitutes for the tool library.

17:33.520 --> 17:34.840
They solve the exact same problem.

17:35.240 --> 17:38.100
So whichever one is tested last looks completely redundant.

17:38.440 --> 17:38.860
Yes.

17:38.860 --> 17:44.460
The researchers attributed a massive leap in AI reasoning to their novel framework, when

17:44.460 --> 17:49.160
a substantial portion of that leap was just the raw, brute force statistical advantage

17:49.160 --> 17:52.520
of rolling the dice 10 times and keeping the highest number.

17:53.280 --> 17:57.600
You know, when you extrapolate this out of the realm of academic AI papers, it becomes a

17:57.600 --> 17:58.140
little terrifying.

17:58.680 --> 17:59.240
How so?

17:59.820 --> 18:00.960
Think about the corporate world.

18:01.080 --> 18:05.680
Think about how many millions of dollars of venture capital funding have been poured into

18:05.680 --> 18:10.780
startups claiming a revolutionary new quote unquote framework, when under the hood, they

18:10.780 --> 18:14.600
are just secretly running a massive portfolio search and cherry picking the best output.

18:15.300 --> 18:16.940
That is a very real concern.

18:17.500 --> 18:20.060
Or considered, you know, a quarterly business report.

18:20.680 --> 18:24.620
The marketing department launches a massive new social media initiative in Q3.

18:25.240 --> 18:27.900
At the end of the quarter, revenue is up 40%.

18:27.900 --> 18:29.240
And they take all the credit.

18:29.760 --> 18:30.240
Exactly.

18:30.860 --> 18:35.000
They sequentially ablate the data, point to the initiative, and claim total victory.

18:35.640 --> 18:37.120
But what else was running in the background?

18:37.760 --> 18:40.360
Did the sales team quietly triple their cold calling quota?

18:40.940 --> 18:43.540
Did a major competitor unexpectedly file for bankruptcy?

18:43.920 --> 18:44.220
Right.

18:44.460 --> 18:49.720
If the executive team doesn't understand interaction effects or hidden search budgets, they will

18:49.720 --> 18:54.720
attribute all that revenue to the social media campaign, double its budget for Q4, and

18:54.720 --> 18:57.240
be completely blindsided when the revenue collapses.

18:57.240 --> 19:01.120
You have to constantly ask, what else was in the background?

19:01.620 --> 19:05.220
Are we mistaking a portfolio effect for a standalone innovation?

19:06.220 --> 19:06.620
Always.

19:07.200 --> 19:10.420
This brings us to a really fascinating shift in Flyxion's essays.

19:10.920 --> 19:15.240
We've explored how researchers can misattribute which parts of their models are working.

19:15.680 --> 19:21.080
But it turns out, the field is also deeply confused about the underlying physics of how these models

19:21.080 --> 19:22.160
navigate problems.

19:22.420 --> 19:24.080
Yes, specifically generative AI.

19:24.080 --> 19:24.520
Right.

19:24.780 --> 19:29.840
We need to talk about this massive wave of generative AI and a pervasive myth about how

19:29.840 --> 19:30.380
it functions.

19:31.180 --> 19:35.160
This is one of the most mathematically satisfying teardowns in the entire collection.

19:35.680 --> 19:39.620
It centers around a very common piece of folklore in the machine learning community.

19:40.120 --> 19:45.300
The slogan goes like this, regression averages, but generation does not.

19:45.300 --> 19:47.840
I have heard this exact phrase in tech talks.

19:48.300 --> 19:50.860
Let me explain the analogy they always use, just for you listening.

19:51.340 --> 19:51.740
Go for it.

19:51.980 --> 19:54.760
Imagine you have an AI robot walking down a long hallway.

19:55.220 --> 19:59.280
At the end of the hallway, there is an open door on the far left and another open door on

19:59.280 --> 19:59.860
the far right.

20:00.420 --> 20:02.120
Between them is a solid brick wall.

20:02.380 --> 20:02.580
Okay.

20:02.900 --> 20:03.480
Classic setup.

20:03.480 --> 20:09.380
The folklore says that if you train the robot using standard regression, the model gets confused

20:09.380 --> 20:10.640
by the two valid options.

20:10.960 --> 20:12.020
It tries to compromise.

20:12.760 --> 20:16.480
It calculates the mathematical average of the left door and the right door, and the robot

20:16.480 --> 20:21.180
confidently marches straight forward, smashing its face right into the solid brick wall.

20:21.760 --> 20:22.620
The averaging trap.

20:22.940 --> 20:23.200
Right.

20:23.640 --> 20:29.360
But if you use a modern generative model, the AI recognizes the multimodal nature of the problem,

20:29.880 --> 20:32.600
randomly selects one of the valid doors, and safely walks through.

20:32.600 --> 20:34.840
That is the canonical narrative.

20:35.480 --> 20:41.620
It is clean, it makes intuitive sense, and as Flyxion meticulously proves, it is fundamentally

20:41.620 --> 20:45.920
flawed in a way that causes researchers to misunderstand their own architecture.

20:46.200 --> 20:47.580
So how does it actually work?

20:48.060 --> 20:52.680
To grasp why, we have to look beneath the hood at the mathematical engine driving the current

20:52.680 --> 20:56.480
state-of-the-art generative models, which are known as flow-matching models.

20:57.420 --> 21:00.780
Flyxion points out this glaring, almost comedic irony.

21:00.780 --> 21:07.240
Flow-matching models, the very systems championed for escaping the averaging trap, are actually

21:07.240 --> 21:09.460
trained using squared error regression.

21:10.100 --> 21:10.960
Wait, stop.

21:11.660 --> 21:12.880
Squared error regression.

21:13.060 --> 21:15.520
I thought the whole point was that they abandoned regression.

21:15.940 --> 21:19.460
I need you to break that down for me because my mental gears just ground to a halt.

21:19.720 --> 21:21.560
They absolutely did not abandon it.

21:21.560 --> 21:26.380
Let's look at what squared error regression actually is in plain terms.

21:27.240 --> 21:32.080
It is an optimization objective that heavily penalizes being far away from the correct answer.

21:32.720 --> 21:32.740
Okay.

21:32.880 --> 21:35.840
If you are two feet off target, your penalty is four.

21:36.300 --> 21:38.980
If you are 10 feet off target, your penalty is 100.

21:39.760 --> 21:45.560
Because it violently hates being severely wrong, the safest statistical strategy when faced with

21:45.560 --> 21:50.580
two distinct, equally likely targets is to hedge your bets and aim right down the middle.

21:50.580 --> 21:52.440
Ah, I see.

21:52.900 --> 21:58.640
Mathematically, the solution that minimizes squared error is always the conditional mean, the average.

21:59.100 --> 21:59.580
Okay.

21:59.680 --> 22:05.000
So if the generative flow-matching model is trained using squared error, its target must also be an average?

22:05.260 --> 22:05.760
Exactly.

22:06.400 --> 22:12.240
The training objective for a flow-matching model is to predict the velocity, the vector pointing

22:12.240 --> 22:15.280
from a sample of pure noise toward a sample of real data.

22:15.280 --> 22:22.480
And the mathematically optimal minimizer for that training objective is unequivocally a conditional

22:22.480 --> 22:23.240
expectation.

22:23.820 --> 22:25.220
So it's an average of velocities.

22:25.560 --> 22:25.780
Yes.

22:26.180 --> 22:31.480
The statement, generation does not average, is mathematically incompatible with the training

22:31.480 --> 22:34.100
objective of the very models people are hyping.

22:34.480 --> 22:36.620
But wait, we know these models work.

22:36.720 --> 22:38.780
I mean, mid-journey generates a coherent image.

22:38.780 --> 22:41.300
It doesn't generate a blurry gray smudge.

22:41.480 --> 22:43.800
The robot does, in fact, walk through the door.

22:43.960 --> 22:46.640
So if it is averaging, why isn't it hitting the wall?

22:47.360 --> 22:50.560
The secret lies in what is being averaged and when.

22:51.060 --> 22:55.740
The old traditional regression models failed because they tried to predict the final endpoint

22:55.740 --> 23:00.160
in one massive blind leap, conditioned only on the starting observation.

23:00.380 --> 23:02.180
They averaged the final destinations.

23:02.500 --> 23:02.760
Right.

23:02.760 --> 23:06.400
The generative flow field is vastly different.

23:06.400 --> 23:10.800
It averages the velocities, but it conditions those velocities on three things.

23:11.500 --> 23:16.100
The initial observation, the current physical position, and the current moment in time.

23:16.580 --> 23:18.120
I think I have an analogy for this.

23:18.220 --> 23:18.960
Tell me if this tracks.

23:19.080 --> 23:19.560
Let's hear it.

23:19.840 --> 23:25.120
The old regression model is like taking a catapult, trying to aim at two different castles simultaneously

23:25.120 --> 23:28.260
and launching a boulder right into the empty fours between them.

23:28.400 --> 23:28.640
Perfect.

23:28.640 --> 23:32.300
But the generative model is like a modern GPS system in your car.

23:32.820 --> 23:36.020
It doesn't teleport you to the destination instantly.

23:36.200 --> 23:40.800
It just calculates the quote-unquote average best direction for the next 50 feet.

23:41.020 --> 23:44.540
You drive forward, then it recalculates based on your new location.

23:44.900 --> 23:46.040
Yes, exactly.

23:46.460 --> 23:50.540
It continuously updates the average velocity at every single intersection

23:50.540 --> 23:54.480
until you are eventually forced to commit to a specific highway on-ramp.

23:54.640 --> 23:57.380
That is a phenomenal translation of the mathematics.

23:57.380 --> 24:01.740
The continuous recalculation based on current position is the key.

24:02.520 --> 24:05.880
Flyxion models this explicitly using a two-mode Gaussian mixture,

24:06.200 --> 24:09.320
which is just math speak for our hallway with the two doors.

24:10.320 --> 24:12.320
Flyxion proves a fascinating point.

24:13.160 --> 24:16.040
If you force the flow-matching model to act like a catapult,

24:16.400 --> 24:22.360
if you make it integrate the entire flow field in a single massive mathematical leap from start to finish,

24:22.820 --> 24:24.440
do you know where the robot lands?

24:24.960 --> 24:26.100
It lands in the wall.

24:26.100 --> 24:28.620
It lands squarely in the wall.

24:29.200 --> 24:33.360
A one-step flow exactly, perfectly reproduces the old regression meme.

24:34.240 --> 24:37.380
The generative sampler has not magically escaped the average.

24:37.860 --> 24:41.040
When restricted to a single step, it is the average.

24:41.480 --> 24:42.160
That's wild.

24:42.620 --> 24:46.180
The true magic of the flow field is not that it abolishes averaging.

24:46.720 --> 24:49.840
It is that it defers and localizes the average over time.

24:49.840 --> 24:52.060
I really want to visualize this deferral.

24:52.240 --> 24:54.120
Let's walk the robot down the hallway.

24:54.720 --> 24:57.040
Time zero is when the robot is standing at the starting line.

24:57.220 --> 24:58.780
What does the average look like there?

24:59.680 --> 25:03.080
At time s equals zero, the very beginning of the trajectory,

25:03.580 --> 25:09.300
your position carries absolutely zero information about which of the two doors you are eventually going to reach.

25:09.620 --> 25:11.460
Because you're equally far from both.

25:11.460 --> 25:12.020
Right.

25:12.380 --> 25:18.220
The mathematical paths leading back from the left door and the right door overlap almost entirely at the starting line.

25:18.920 --> 25:25.200
Because of this profound ambiguity, the optimal velocity field at the beginning actually does point straight down the middle.

25:25.580 --> 25:27.340
It points toward the global average.

25:27.980 --> 25:28.980
It points at the wall.

25:28.980 --> 25:30.400
That is crazy.

25:30.800 --> 25:33.280
It starts out making the exact same mistake.

25:33.560 --> 25:33.860
Yes.

25:34.660 --> 25:40.800
But as time progresses and the robot moves forward, the paths leading to the two doors begin to diverge in space.

25:41.560 --> 25:47.000
Flyxion calculates an analytic threshold where this divergence becomes mathematically distinguishable.

25:47.680 --> 25:47.980
Okay.

25:48.080 --> 25:49.440
So it starts to become clear.

25:49.440 --> 25:56.680
In their specific model, this happens around halfway down the hall at time sc equals 0.5.

25:56.680 --> 26:00.160
Before this point, the destination is ambiguous.

26:00.780 --> 26:04.640
But once you cross this threshold, the flow field sharply resolves.

26:05.200 --> 26:06.320
Meaning it picks a side.

26:06.660 --> 26:11.100
If the noise happens to push the robot even a fraction of an inch to the left of the center line,

26:11.640 --> 26:16.120
the updated conditional average velocity overwhelmingly pulls it toward the left door.

26:16.660 --> 26:19.360
The averaging process never stops functioning.

26:19.480 --> 26:26.640
It's just that the pool of potential destinations being averaged shrinks from both doors to just the left door.

26:27.300 --> 26:29.320
Based on the robot's evolving physical position.

26:30.020 --> 26:32.020
The ambiguity is resolved dynamically.

26:32.840 --> 26:38.740
So the entire marketing narrative around generative AI avoiding the average is just like sloppy philosophy.

26:38.960 --> 26:40.560
It's not a new paradigm of reasoning.

26:40.700 --> 26:42.820
It's just taking smaller steps and recalculating.

26:43.220 --> 26:43.620
Exactly.

26:44.060 --> 26:50.720
And relying on that sloppy philosophy leads researchers to publish entirely false conclusions about why their systems succeed.

26:50.720 --> 26:51.180
Right.

26:51.180 --> 26:58.660
We can actually return to that active 3D mapping robotics paper, the Lee et al. paper, to see this happen in real time.

26:59.640 --> 27:04.540
The authors claim that their flow-driven multi-anchor generation module was brilliant,

27:04.960 --> 27:10.980
specifically because it proposed multiple distinct paths, thereby avoiding the collapse of deterministic regression.

27:10.980 --> 27:11.660
Right.

27:11.660 --> 27:16.240
They claim that by giving the robot five different anchors to choose from, they defeated the averaging trap.

27:16.700 --> 27:18.360
That was the narrative in the published text.

27:19.420 --> 27:26.320
But Flyxion, being incredibly thorough, dug into the extended 42-page preprint version of the paper.

27:26.940 --> 27:30.380
And buried in the supplemental data was a control experiment.

27:30.380 --> 27:32.040
Oh, I love it.

27:32.300 --> 27:32.900
What did they find?

27:33.420 --> 27:39.080
The researchers tested what happens if you restrict the generative model to sample only a single anchor.

27:39.540 --> 27:41.260
Mathematically, k equals 1.

27:41.940 --> 27:43.220
No multiple alternatives.

27:43.740 --> 27:45.940
No retaining of diverse hypotheses.

27:46.480 --> 27:51.160
So they essentially forced the generative model to act just like the old deterministic model,

27:51.360 --> 27:53.260
outputting only one single guess.

27:53.620 --> 27:54.540
What happened to the score?

27:55.020 --> 27:58.700
Well, the old regression baseline scored 62.47.

27:58.700 --> 28:03.720
The full generative model with five anchors scored 68.45.

28:04.160 --> 28:04.440
Okay.

28:04.660 --> 28:11.780
But the generative model restricted to just one single anchor still achieved a score of 64.92.

28:12.220 --> 28:13.980
Okay, let me do the math on that real quick.

28:14.100 --> 28:19.740
The total performance jump from the old way to the new five-anchor way is 5.98 points.

28:20.080 --> 28:20.340
Right.

28:20.540 --> 28:24.480
But just switching to the generative architecture, even when handicapped to a single guess,

28:24.840 --> 28:27.120
gives you a jump of 2.45 points.

28:27.120 --> 28:29.480
That is 41% of the total improvement.

28:30.120 --> 28:30.280
Yes.

28:31.060 --> 28:36.840
A massive 41% of the performance gain was present even when the model was completely prohibited

28:36.840 --> 28:38.820
from generating multiple alternatives.

28:39.080 --> 28:41.580
So it wasn't about the multiple options at all.

28:42.020 --> 28:42.380
Exactly.

28:42.920 --> 28:48.640
You absolutely cannot attribute that portion of the gain to retaining multiple hypotheses

28:48.640 --> 28:51.560
because there was only one hypothesis.

28:51.560 --> 28:53.820
That completely shatters their thesis.

28:54.660 --> 28:57.900
What is actually causing that 41% bump then?

28:58.400 --> 29:00.760
It is almost certainly the mechanics we just discussed.

29:01.280 --> 29:04.120
The iterative nature of the flow-matching architecture.

29:04.520 --> 29:05.880
The way it localizes the average.

29:06.460 --> 29:08.900
The stochasticity injected during the steps.

29:09.440 --> 29:15.240
But because the authors were so blinded by the philosophical slogan that generation does not average,

29:15.240 --> 29:20.620
they attributed the entire conceptual victory to avoiding hypothesis collapse.

29:21.180 --> 29:22.320
They just wrote a good story.

29:22.680 --> 29:23.040
Basically.

29:24.240 --> 29:29.420
Flyxion points out that to scientifically prove their claim, they needed a totally different control.

29:29.920 --> 29:34.080
They needed to test other, less complex methods of proposing multiple paths,

29:34.080 --> 29:39.420
like just pulling five feasible goals from the frontier of the map using a traditional heuristic

29:39.420 --> 29:44.320
to see if the complex flow-matching math actually had a comparative advantage

29:44.320 --> 29:48.380
or if simply asking the system for five random guesses is all it took.

29:49.180 --> 29:51.920
It feels like an epidemic of narrative-driven science.

29:52.400 --> 29:56.220
We invent a beautiful philosophical reason for why our new code works

29:56.220 --> 29:59.460
and we just ignore the math, sitting quietly in the appendix screaming,

29:59.680 --> 30:01.360
no, you just took more steps.

30:01.600 --> 30:02.280
It really is.

30:02.280 --> 30:08.260
And Flyxion highlights one final, exquisite irony about the mathematics of flow-matching.

30:08.500 --> 30:09.120
Ooh, what is it?

30:09.620 --> 30:13.680
We discussed how the robot takes continuous small steps to update the average.

30:14.500 --> 30:18.220
But computers cannot process truly continuous time.

30:18.580 --> 30:24.440
They have to approximate it using finite, discrete steps, a process called numerical integration.

30:25.160 --> 30:27.560
Often, they use something called an Euler step.

30:28.180 --> 30:28.540
Okay.

30:28.540 --> 30:34.380
What do you think happens on the very last Euler step, right as the robot reaches the door?

30:34.880 --> 30:37.840
Hmm, does the discrete math mess up the trajectory?

30:37.840 --> 30:40.680
It does something deeply poetic.

30:41.780 --> 30:50.420
Flyxion mathematically demonstrates that the final step of the numerical integrator is inherently a partial move toward a posterior mean.

30:51.060 --> 30:55.900
If the size of that final time step is too large relative to the physical spread of the target data,

30:55.900 --> 31:01.800
the algorithm mathematically forces the output to collapse back toward the center of the mode.

31:01.800 --> 31:04.200
You have got to be kidding me.

31:04.320 --> 31:12.580
You build this incredibly complex, computationally expensive generative architecture specifically to escape the gravitational pull of the average.

31:13.040 --> 31:13.220
Yep.

31:13.380 --> 31:19.660
And the literal last line of code in the mathematical solver sneaks the average right back into the system.

31:19.820 --> 31:22.940
It is the perfect encapsulation of Flyxion's broader point.

31:22.940 --> 31:28.420
You cannot escape the fundamental laws of statistics just by rebranding your architecture.

31:28.740 --> 31:30.620
The math will always assert itself.

31:31.640 --> 31:32.800
This is a perfect pivot point.

31:33.240 --> 31:36.260
We've seen how researchers misinterpret their ablation tables,

31:36.600 --> 31:40.740
and we've explored how they misunderstand the internal physics of their generative models.

31:41.440 --> 31:45.440
But what happens if the ruler they are using to measure all of this is fundamentally broken?

31:45.700 --> 31:46.080
Yes.

31:46.280 --> 31:48.440
We are moving from the model to the metric.

31:48.440 --> 31:52.620
This touches on a deeply pervasive issue in modern benchmarking.

31:53.180 --> 31:59.580
In experimental sciences like chemistry or biology, if you invent a new test, you must validate it using controls.

31:59.720 --> 32:00.520
Right. Like a placebo?

32:00.920 --> 32:01.280
Exactly.

32:01.760 --> 32:06.040
You run a negative control, where you know the signal is absent, to establish a baseline.

32:06.640 --> 32:11.000
Crucially, you also run a positive control, where you know the signal is definitely present.

32:11.260 --> 32:11.760
Makes sense.

32:11.760 --> 32:18.540
If your test cannot distinguish between the negative and positive control, your test is blind, and you throw it in the trash.

32:19.520 --> 32:23.740
But in machine learning, researchers often only utilize negative controls.

32:24.260 --> 32:27.980
They compare their complex new model against a trivial, brain-dead baseline.

32:28.540 --> 32:33.520
And when the complex model loses to the brain-dead baseline, the researchers face a crisis of faith.

32:34.100 --> 32:38.300
Do they admit their model is useless, or do they claim the grading rubric is flawed?

32:39.000 --> 32:41.340
History suggests they often blame the rubric.

32:42.120 --> 32:46.960
Flyxion examines a recent brief communication paper focused on genetic perturbation models.

32:47.300 --> 32:48.880
Okay. Let's give some context for that.

32:48.880 --> 32:57.700
These are AI models trying to predict how individual biological cells will react when you perturb their genes, say, by introducing a drug.

32:58.420 --> 33:07.880
The field had been facing a crisis because highly complex, deep-learning architectures were routinely failing to beat an uninformative mean baseline.

33:07.880 --> 33:13.320
The baseline was literally just guessing the average behavior of the cells, and the AI couldn't beat it?

33:13.540 --> 33:13.780
Right.

33:14.320 --> 33:22.180
So the authors of this new paper decided to prove that the AI was actually brilliant, but the old metrics were just blind.

33:22.180 --> 33:23.960
I see where this is going.

33:23.960 --> 33:29.700
To their credit, they introduced a positive control, which they termed a technical duplicate.

33:30.600 --> 33:36.200
And to prove their point, they invented a brand-new metric called the dynamic range fraction, or DRF.

33:36.200 --> 33:39.260
Let's break down the architecture of this DRF metric.

33:39.380 --> 33:40.540
How is it actually calculated?

33:41.060 --> 33:42.780
DRF is essentially a ratio.

33:43.720 --> 33:49.360
The numerator, the top half of the fraction, measures the gap between the positive control and the negative baseline.

33:50.060 --> 33:55.800
It asks, how well can this metric separate a known brilliant signal from a known garbage signal?

33:55.800 --> 34:04.380
The denominator, the bottom half of the fraction, measures the gap between a theoretically perfect score and the negative baseline.

34:05.000 --> 34:07.440
It represents the total possible runway.

34:08.080 --> 34:14.440
So the DRF metric calculates what fraction of the ideal perfect range your model is successfully capturing.

34:14.580 --> 34:14.940
Exactly.

34:15.340 --> 34:17.320
Conceptually, that sounds incredibly robust.

34:17.420 --> 34:18.680
I mean, it makes total sense.

34:19.280 --> 34:20.920
Where is the mathematical trapdoor?

34:20.920 --> 34:26.760
The trapdoor is hidden in the denominator, the assumption of a perfect score.

34:27.640 --> 34:34.400
Flyxion builds a toy model to demonstrate why achieving that perfect score is a mathematical impossibility in this specific context.

34:34.700 --> 34:35.380
Why is it impossible?

34:36.240 --> 34:43.020
You see, the ground truth these models are trying to predict is not some flawless, abstract, mathematical absolute.

34:43.020 --> 34:51.400
The ground truth is derived from a physical, biological sample, usually the mean expression profile of a small, finite number of cells.

34:52.020 --> 34:55.160
Let's say a sample of 100 cells.

34:55.820 --> 35:02.060
And because it is a physical sample of only 100 cells, it is going to have inherent biological and statistical noise.

35:02.120 --> 35:02.700
Precisely.

35:03.040 --> 35:05.340
The answer key itself is blurry.

35:05.800 --> 35:06.040
Right.

35:06.800 --> 35:08.800
Flyxion runs a mathematical simulation.

35:09.800 --> 35:11.360
Imagine an oracle model.

35:11.360 --> 35:14.160
This oracle is magically omniscient.

35:14.520 --> 35:21.040
It knows the exact, true, underlying biological profile of the cell population with absolute zero error.

35:21.420 --> 35:22.040
It's perfect.

35:22.500 --> 35:26.560
But it is still graded against the noisy sample of 100 cells.

35:27.580 --> 35:36.580
Flyxion calculates that under standard squared error evaluation, this magically perfect oracle would only achieve a DRF score of about 0.13.

35:36.960 --> 35:37.320
Wait, wait, wait.

35:37.760 --> 35:39.220
Let me process that.

35:39.220 --> 35:49.980
So, the metric assumes that a perfect model will score 1.0, but the statistical noise in the answer key mathematically caps the maximum possible score at 0.13.

35:49.980 --> 35:50.660
Yes.

35:51.660 --> 35:54.360
The ceiling of reality is 13%.

35:54.360 --> 35:57.820
So, imagine a researcher testing a new AI model.

35:58.140 --> 35:59.160
They run the test.

35:59.380 --> 36:01.540
They get a DRF score of 0.13.

36:01.820 --> 36:04.420
And they look at the denominator of 1.0.

36:05.140 --> 36:07.360
They conclude, this metric is terrible.

36:07.660 --> 36:12.420
My model is a genius, but the metric is only capturing 13% of the total available signal.

36:12.420 --> 36:16.420
When, in reality, the metric is performing flawlessly.

36:16.640 --> 36:20.440
It is capturing 100% of the actually available signal.

36:20.840 --> 36:21.220
Exactly.

36:21.980 --> 36:27.860
The shortfall is an optical illusion created by setting the denominator to an impossible mathematical fantasy.

36:28.260 --> 36:34.480
You know, I want to translate this to a totally different world so you listening can feel the pain of this error.

36:34.480 --> 36:38.980
Imagine you are a manager evaluating KPIs for your sales team.

36:39.120 --> 36:39.920
This will be good.

36:40.160 --> 36:42.800
You set the target conversion rate at 100%.

36:42.800 --> 36:46.540
You expect every single person who clicks on the website to buy a product.

36:46.880 --> 36:53.220
But mathematically, because of random market noise, bots, and, you know, irreducible customer churn,

36:53.700 --> 36:58.620
the absolute maximum possible conversion rate in your industry is 13%.

36:58.620 --> 37:05.160
If you judge your team against the 100% denominator, you are going to constantly yell at them,

37:05.360 --> 37:07.100
fire people, and burn through consultants,

37:07.560 --> 37:12.880
completely unaware that your team is actually performing at absolute mathematically optimal perfection.

37:13.240 --> 37:16.680
A shortfall relative to fantasy is not a failure.

37:17.020 --> 37:18.840
That is a brilliant translation.

37:19.760 --> 37:22.980
You are punishing reality for not being a eutopia.

37:23.860 --> 37:26.400
But the genetic perturbation paper goes even further.

37:26.400 --> 37:30.480
And this is where the cure becomes much worse than the disease.

37:31.020 --> 37:31.280
Uh-oh.

37:31.920 --> 37:35.060
To fix the perceived blindness of the traditional metrics,

37:35.400 --> 37:38.300
which were failing to capture that missing 87%,

37:38.300 --> 37:41.440
the authors proposed using a weighted metric.

37:42.080 --> 37:47.040
They decided to concentrate the metric's attention heavily on the specific subset of genes

37:47.040 --> 37:49.580
that were known to be affected by the perturbation,

37:49.960 --> 37:51.760
while down-weighting everything else.

37:51.940 --> 37:53.540
I think I see the danger here.

37:53.540 --> 37:56.800
If you design the test so it only looks at the things you know changed,

37:56.920 --> 37:59.280
your score is artificially going to skyrocket.

37:59.920 --> 38:00.980
It did skyrocket.

38:01.400 --> 38:04.880
The weighted metric beautifully separated the positive and negative controls.

38:05.520 --> 38:09.280
But Flyxion introduces a vital concept called a failure control

38:09.280 --> 38:12.500
to expose the catastrophic vulnerability of this approach.

38:12.960 --> 38:14.200
What's a failure control?

38:14.880 --> 38:16.960
Imagine you build a rogue AI model.

38:17.380 --> 38:20.300
This model perfectly predicts the handful of affected genes,

38:20.300 --> 38:25.960
but then it completely loses its mind and outputs absolute massive random noise

38:25.960 --> 38:29.260
across the thousands of other unaffected genes in the cell.

38:29.620 --> 38:31.820
It is a biological catastrophe.

38:32.640 --> 38:36.280
And how does the new weighted metric grade this catastrophic failure?

38:36.840 --> 38:39.680
Well, under the old standard squared error metric,

38:40.080 --> 38:42.100
this rogue model was heavily penalized.

38:42.480 --> 38:45.720
It scored 6.7 times worse than the brain-dead baseline.

38:45.720 --> 38:48.960
But under the author's new weighted metric,

38:49.420 --> 38:52.540
because it ignored the noise and only looked to the affected genes,

38:52.980 --> 38:55.920
the rogue model scored 60% better than the baseline.

38:56.660 --> 38:57.120
You're kidding.

38:57.640 --> 39:01.720
The metric declared the catastrophic failure to be a massive scientific breakthrough.

39:02.100 --> 39:05.840
It is the equivalent of a history professor handing out a final exam.

39:06.260 --> 39:09.180
A student only studies for three questions out of 100.

39:09.180 --> 39:11.300
They get those three perfectly right,

39:11.580 --> 39:15.920
and they just draw random squiggles and curse words for the other 97 questions.

39:17.200 --> 39:22.760
If the professor uses a weighted metric that only grades the three questions they studied for,

39:22.940 --> 39:24.580
the student looks like a valedictorian.

39:25.100 --> 39:27.400
But if you look at the entire exam,

39:27.940 --> 39:30.980
their actual signal is overwhelmingly diluted by their noise.

39:31.340 --> 39:32.600
That is it exactly.

39:33.060 --> 39:36.140
And that is Phlyxian's ultimate decree on metrics.

39:36.140 --> 39:38.580
If you are going to calibrate a metric,

39:39.060 --> 39:41.680
you cannot just use a negative and positive control.

39:41.900 --> 39:43.620
You must run a failure control.

39:44.200 --> 39:47.900
You have to definitively prove that your metric is sensitive to the true signal

39:47.900 --> 39:52.100
without simultaneously rendering itself blind to catastrophic noise.

39:53.040 --> 39:57.180
This theme of researchers accidentally designing the test so they automatically win,

39:57.740 --> 40:00.060
it brings us to another paper of Phlyxian critiques.

40:00.260 --> 40:05.340
It is about nonlinear oscillators and the concept of modulatability.

40:05.340 --> 40:09.560
I want to dive into this because it shows how the physical protocol of an experiment

40:09.560 --> 40:12.280
can draw a literal picture of an illusion.

40:13.080 --> 40:16.300
Yes, this is a masterclass in experimental confounding.

40:16.800 --> 40:19.660
The paper explores a complex network problem.

40:20.200 --> 40:23.780
Imagine a massive web of interconnected oscillating nodes,

40:23.900 --> 40:27.160
like the power grid or a neural network in the brain.

40:27.620 --> 40:27.900
Okay.

40:28.140 --> 40:30.500
The researchers asked an inverse problem.

40:30.500 --> 40:34.140
If I want to change specific properties of this network,

40:34.760 --> 40:38.800
say, I want to alter the amplitude of 50 specific nodes,

40:39.360 --> 40:45.460
how many individual connections or edges do I need to reach in and manually modulate

40:45.460 --> 40:47.280
to achieve that precise control?

40:47.280 --> 40:51.100
It's like standing in front of a massive audio mixing board.

40:51.100 --> 40:53.580
You have a specific sound you want to create in the room,

40:53.740 --> 40:57.620
and you are asking how many different knobs you need to twist simultaneously

40:57.620 --> 41:00.640
to force the speakers to output that exact frequency.

41:01.140 --> 41:02.220
That is a great visual.

41:02.740 --> 41:08.380
To answer this, the author cited a classical mathematical theorem regarding Jacobian matrices.

41:09.320 --> 41:10.620
Jacobian matrices.

41:10.620 --> 41:14.380
A Jacobian matrix is essentially a mathematical grid

41:14.380 --> 41:18.780
that tracks how much one variable in a complex system changes

41:18.780 --> 41:21.080
when you subtly tweak another variable.

41:21.720 --> 41:24.880
The theorem states that to achieve local uniqueness,

41:25.380 --> 41:28.180
meaning you have a clean, precise mathematical solution,

41:28.820 --> 41:32.980
you generally need as many free parameters as you have target properties.

41:33.360 --> 41:34.420
Dimensions must match.

41:34.640 --> 41:34.920
Right.

41:35.100 --> 41:39.120
If you want to control 50 target nodes, you must modulate 50 edges.

41:39.120 --> 41:41.500
To prove this theorem empirically,

41:42.000 --> 41:45.560
the researchers ran a massive simulation and plotted a heat map.

41:46.020 --> 41:50.000
The horizontal x-axis was the number of targets they wanted to control.

41:50.540 --> 41:54.160
The vertical y-axis was the number of edges they modulated.

41:54.460 --> 41:56.720
And I'm guessing the heat map looked perfect.

41:57.060 --> 42:01.960
It was a beautiful, bright ridge of success marching perfectly along the diagonal.

42:02.500 --> 42:07.120
When they wanted 50 targets, success spiked precisely when they modulated 50 edges.

42:07.120 --> 42:10.640
75 targets required 75 edges.

42:11.000 --> 42:11.260
Wow.

42:11.660 --> 42:17.320
It looked like an absolute stunning empirical validation of the Jacobian dimension matching theorem.

42:17.800 --> 42:21.460
Whenever you say beautiful and stunning, I know a trap door isn't about to open.

42:22.040 --> 42:22.660
What did they miss?

42:22.860 --> 42:26.420
They misunderstood what the mathematical theorem actually guarantees.

42:27.000 --> 42:30.920
The theorem only dictates how many solutions you should expect to exist locally,

42:31.460 --> 42:34.460
assuming the Jacobian matrix is full rank and well behaved.

42:34.460 --> 42:40.500
It does absolutely nothing to guarantee that you can easily find those solutions.

42:41.180 --> 42:44.260
And it certainly does not guarantee that randomly perturbing the network

42:44.260 --> 42:47.000
will magically hit a specific success threshold.

42:47.660 --> 42:52.560
So, Flyxion looked past the math and examined the literal physical code

42:52.560 --> 42:54.560
of how the experiment was assigned.

42:54.840 --> 42:56.900
The protocol of the simulation itself.

42:57.280 --> 42:57.580
Yes.

42:57.580 --> 43:03.060
The researchers used an assignment rule that Flyxion calls targets-first sorting.

43:03.620 --> 43:07.480
When setting up the simulation, they first generated a list of all the nodes in the network.

43:08.000 --> 43:12.120
The first M nodes on that list were designated as the target nodes.

43:12.380 --> 43:12.680
Right.

43:12.980 --> 43:18.980
Then, they took the first E nodes on that same exact list and directly perturbed their incoming edges.

43:19.440 --> 43:19.720
Wait.

43:19.940 --> 43:20.320
Pause.

43:21.080 --> 43:22.160
Let me process this.

43:22.160 --> 43:26.080
If they set their targets to 50, they picked nodes 1 through 50.

43:26.340 --> 43:31.480
If they modulated 50 edges, they modulated the edges connected to nodes 1 through 50.

43:31.800 --> 43:32.140
Yes.

43:32.660 --> 43:37.700
They were directly twisting the knobs of the exact same speakers they were trying to measure.

43:38.620 --> 43:39.180
Yes.

43:39.560 --> 43:41.820
Because of the target's first sorting protocol,

43:41.820 --> 43:44.820
a target node is directly, physically perturbed,

43:44.940 --> 43:51.520
if, and only if, its index number is less than or equal to the total number of edges modulated.

43:52.200 --> 43:52.760
That's insane.

43:53.200 --> 43:56.040
Obviously, if you directly hit the node you were trying to change,

43:56.300 --> 43:57.980
its amplitude is going to change.

43:58.800 --> 44:02.080
The brilliant diagonal ridge of success on the heat map

44:02.080 --> 44:04.900
was not a profound confirmation of Jacobian mathematics.

44:05.580 --> 44:09.640
It was a completely trivial artifact of the fact that they sorted the list.

44:10.600 --> 44:12.020
That is staggering.

44:12.880 --> 44:14.980
That is the equivalent of a scientist saying,

44:14.980 --> 44:19.900
I have a complex theory that throwing rocks at glass windows will cause them to shatter.

44:20.380 --> 44:23.960
To prove this empirically, I will select 10 windows on this building as my targets.

44:24.360 --> 44:28.500
I will then throw exactly 10 rocks directly at those specific 10 windows.

44:28.900 --> 44:31.020
Behold, a 100% correlation.

44:31.440 --> 44:32.640
The math is flawless.

44:33.160 --> 44:34.900
It is exactly like that.

44:35.500 --> 44:37.900
It is a tautology disguised as an experiment.

44:38.840 --> 44:42.480
And Flyxion goes one step further to prove the fragility of the illusion.

44:42.480 --> 44:46.860
They build a toy network model and introduce the concept of propagation.

44:47.400 --> 44:50.780
We're hitting one node since a ripple effect that alters its neighbors.

44:51.300 --> 44:51.860
Like a shockwave.

44:52.140 --> 44:53.320
If propagation is strong,

44:53.800 --> 44:57.460
targets that you didn't directly hit with a rock will still shatter from the shockwave.

44:58.180 --> 45:00.680
When Flyxion runs the heat map with propagation,

45:01.260 --> 45:03.400
do you know what happens to that beautiful diagonal ridge?

45:03.720 --> 45:05.980
The ridge completely moves off the diagonal line.

45:05.980 --> 45:08.140
It drops drastically.

45:08.860 --> 45:14.960
With high propagation, you might only need to modulate 26 edges to successfully change 50 target nodes.

45:15.480 --> 45:20.400
The position of that ridge is entirely dependent on the physical coupling strength of the network

45:20.400 --> 45:23.580
and the arbitrary sorting rules of the Python code,

45:23.900 --> 45:26.780
not on a fundamental mathematical theorem about dimensions.

45:26.780 --> 45:30.080
This is exactly why I find these essays so vital.

45:30.480 --> 45:35.200
We are peeling back the pristine, intimidating layers of published science

45:35.200 --> 45:37.860
to see how the evidence is physically constructed.

45:38.100 --> 45:41.200
Which brings us to the final major theme of our deep dive today.

45:41.800 --> 45:42.540
The statistics.

45:42.980 --> 45:43.220
Right.

45:43.480 --> 45:47.340
We are moving away from complex AI architectures and niche biology

45:47.340 --> 45:51.000
and zooming out to the most universal language of science itself,

45:51.540 --> 45:52.680
basic statistics.

45:52.680 --> 45:56.200
We are talking about error bars, p-values, and log scales.

45:57.100 --> 46:00.080
This is perhaps the most crucial section for the general listener,

46:00.420 --> 46:06.700
because these are the statistical tools that even seasoned peer-reviewing experts get routinely tricked by.

46:07.680 --> 46:11.320
Flyxion systematically dismantles two golden rules of thumb

46:11.320 --> 46:14.620
that are taught in almost every introductory statistics class,

46:15.500 --> 46:19.880
demonstrating why they are dangerously misleading in the context of modern research.

46:20.360 --> 46:21.540
Let's take them one by one.

46:21.540 --> 46:24.720
I want to start with error bars, because as a layperson,

46:24.940 --> 46:28.040
I was taught a very specific hard and fast rule in college.

46:28.460 --> 46:29.240
Oh, the overlap rule.

46:29.500 --> 46:29.740
Yeah.

46:30.000 --> 46:31.300
You look at a bar chart.

46:31.760 --> 46:34.080
Method A has a performance score of 80,

46:34.300 --> 46:37.860
and the little error bar line stretches from 75 to 85.

46:38.500 --> 46:43.180
Method B has a score of 90, with an error bar stretching from 85 to 95.

46:43.580 --> 46:45.480
The rule of thumb I was taught is,

46:45.600 --> 46:49.280
if the visual ranges of those error bars overlap even slightly,

46:49.280 --> 46:53.660
the results are a statistical tie, you cannot claim one method is better.

46:54.380 --> 46:55.560
That's what everyone is taught.

46:56.040 --> 46:59.600
But Flyxion's essay completely shattered my confidence in that.

47:00.100 --> 47:02.940
Why is the visual overlap test failing us?

47:03.100 --> 47:06.520
It fails because it makes intuitive visual sense,

47:07.000 --> 47:11.160
but it is mathematically inappropriate for how we actually conduct tests.

47:11.160 --> 47:14.240
Let's break it down into two layers of failure.

47:14.960 --> 47:17.160
First, even in the simplest scenario,

47:17.340 --> 47:19.740
where the two sets of data are completely independent,

47:20.300 --> 47:23.860
the non-overlap rule is mathematically far too conservative.

47:24.340 --> 47:25.140
How conservative?

47:25.140 --> 47:29.200
A standard error bar represents a 95% confidence interval.

47:29.960 --> 47:34.360
But demanding that two 95% intervals completely clear each other without touching

47:34.360 --> 47:37.520
does not equate to a 5% significance test.

47:38.400 --> 47:43.440
Flyxion shows that it actually equates to a 0.56% significance test.

47:43.840 --> 47:44.080
Whoa.

47:44.780 --> 47:48.780
So it is nearly 10 times harder to pass the visual overlap test

47:48.780 --> 47:52.660
than it is to pass a proper mathematically rigorous statistical test?

47:52.660 --> 47:56.720
Yes. It is heavily biased toward declaring a false tie.

47:57.420 --> 48:01.580
But it gets exponentially worse when we introduce the concept of paired tests,

48:01.940 --> 48:04.640
which are the standard in machine learning benchmarks.

48:05.020 --> 48:06.400
Paired tests. Explain that.

48:06.800 --> 48:09.220
When researchers test method A and method B,

48:09.740 --> 48:12.840
they don't test method A on a random batch of 200 images

48:12.840 --> 48:16.240
and method B on a completely different batch of 200 images.

48:16.700 --> 48:21.400
To make it a fair fight, they test both methods on the exact same 200 images.

48:21.400 --> 48:25.160
Right. That ensures that one method didn't accidentally get an easier test.

48:25.320 --> 48:25.640
Exactly.

48:26.240 --> 48:29.160
But because they are evaluated on the identical items,

48:29.540 --> 48:32.040
the inherent difficulty of those items is shared.

48:32.660 --> 48:37.240
Let's say image number 42 is an incredibly dark, blurry, difficult scene.

48:38.100 --> 48:42.540
Both method A and method B are going to perform poorly on image 42.

48:43.220 --> 48:48.200
That shared difficulty creates a massive statistical correlation between the two methods.

48:48.200 --> 48:51.360
Okay. I followed the logic. How does that break the error bar?

48:51.840 --> 48:55.860
When you run a proper mathematical paired statistical test,

48:56.140 --> 48:58.100
that shared variance is factored out.

48:58.920 --> 49:03.040
You subtract method B's score from method A's score on image 42.

49:03.820 --> 49:07.320
Because the dark and blurry penalty hit both of them equally,

49:07.580 --> 49:09.880
it mathematically cancels out in the difference.

49:09.980 --> 49:10.720
Oh, I see.

49:10.720 --> 49:13.980
This makes the standard error of the difference very small,

49:14.260 --> 49:19.480
granting you a highly precise, razor-sharp measurement of which method is actually superior.

49:19.780 --> 49:23.620
But the visual error bars drawn on the chart don't know that the data was paired.

49:23.920 --> 49:24.420
Precisely.

49:24.860 --> 49:28.940
The individual error bars plotted on the paper are marginal intervals.

49:29.420 --> 49:32.780
They still contain all the massive variance of the scene difficulty.

49:32.980 --> 49:33.780
They don't shrink.

49:33.780 --> 49:37.320
So, as the shared difficulty of the test items increases,

49:37.900 --> 49:42.760
those individual error bars get wider and wider, overlapping more and more visually.

49:43.080 --> 49:43.560
Wow.

49:44.280 --> 49:48.920
Meanwhile, the true paired difference between the methods remains crystal clear

49:48.920 --> 49:51.100
and highly statistically significant.

49:51.100 --> 49:56.560
So, we are potentially looking at published papers containing massive technological breakthroughs,

49:56.860 --> 49:58.000
looking at the charts and saying,

49:58.140 --> 50:00.080
oh, the bars overlap, no improvement here.

50:00.240 --> 50:02.980
When in reality, if you analyze the paired data,

50:03.340 --> 50:06.060
method A demolished method B on every single test.

50:06.380 --> 50:08.760
It is a statistical tragedy.

50:09.100 --> 50:11.900
Flyxion runs a simulation with 200 shared items.

50:12.440 --> 50:14.540
In a scenario with high shared difficulty,

50:14.840 --> 50:20.480
the proper paired t-test correctly identifies the superior method 98% of the time.

50:21.100 --> 50:24.520
Do you know how often the visual non-overlap rule catches the winner?

50:25.040 --> 50:27.100
Based on your tone, I'm guessing close to zero?

50:27.520 --> 50:28.060
Zero percent.

50:28.420 --> 50:31.080
It catches it zero percent of the time.

50:31.760 --> 50:35.300
The visual rule has zero statistical power in that environment.

50:35.680 --> 50:36.860
That is deeply frustrating.

50:37.520 --> 50:39.140
Okay, so we've dismantled the first rule.

50:39.340 --> 50:42.360
What is the second golden rule of stats that we need to unlearn?

50:42.840 --> 50:43.580
The converse error.

50:44.300 --> 50:50.500
Treating a microscopic p-value as absolute incontrovertible proof that a method is universally better.

50:51.100 --> 50:52.400
You see this constantly.

50:53.060 --> 50:53.760
A paper declares,

50:54.260 --> 50:58.600
our new method beats the baseline with a p-value of 10 to the negative 10th.

50:58.720 --> 50:59.200
Right.

50:59.460 --> 51:01.680
The reader naturally assumes that the debate is over.

51:01.900 --> 51:03.520
It's practically a law of physics.

51:03.760 --> 51:08.720
Right, because a p-value that tiny implies the odds of the result being a fluke are astronomically low.

51:08.820 --> 51:10.880
You can't argue with 10 to the negative 10th.

51:10.880 --> 51:18.260
You can't argue with the math of the p-value, but you must aggressively argue with what the p-value is actually measuring.

51:19.000 --> 51:21.360
Think about how a machine learning model is created.

51:21.820 --> 51:22.640
It is trained.

51:23.200 --> 51:26.740
That training process involves immense randomness.

51:27.340 --> 51:28.700
Random initialization weights.

51:29.120 --> 51:30.600
Random shuffling of the training data.

51:30.820 --> 51:31.020
Sure.

51:31.320 --> 51:32.060
Lots of variables.

51:32.060 --> 51:48.740
If you evaluate one single trained instance, one checkpoint of method A, and one single trained checkpoint of method B, on a massive benchmark of 20,000 test items, and run a paired test, you will almost certainly generate a microscopic p-value.

51:49.600 --> 51:59.400
With 20,000 items, you have the immense resolution required to detect even the most microscopic, trivial difference between those two specific blocks of code.

51:59.400 --> 52:02.880
But isn't detecting a difference the entire point of the benchmark?

52:03.760 --> 52:07.980
It is the point if your goal is to claim that checkpoint A is better than checkpoint B.

52:08.620 --> 52:18.080
It is absolutely invalid if your goal is to claim that the underlying concept of method A is universally superior to the concept of method B.

52:18.980 --> 52:19.280
Oh.

52:19.680 --> 52:24.520
Because if I retrained the exact same models tomorrow, the random variants might flip the results.

52:25.060 --> 52:26.540
Let me try an analogy here.

52:27.000 --> 52:27.540
Let's hear it.

52:27.540 --> 52:33.440
Imagine I want to prove that professional sprinters are fundamentally faster than amateur runners.

52:33.880 --> 52:36.740
I set up a race between Usain Bolt and an amateur.

52:37.520 --> 52:45.560
However, unbeknownst to me, Usain Bolt was randomly assigned to wear lead shoes that morning, and the amateur was wearing high-tech track spikes.

52:45.920 --> 52:46.280
Okay.

52:46.620 --> 52:47.100
They race.

52:47.720 --> 52:48.460
The amateur wins.

52:48.460 --> 52:54.960
To make my data rigorous, I have them race against each other on 20,000 different tracks around the world.

52:55.480 --> 53:00.640
Because the amateur has the spikes and Bolt has the lead shoes, the amateur wins every single time.

53:01.220 --> 53:02.460
I calculate my p-value.

53:02.880 --> 53:04.600
It is 10 to the negative 10th.

53:04.800 --> 53:04.940
Yep.

53:04.940 --> 53:12.080
I publish a paper claiming, with absolute statistical certainty, that amateurs are fundamentally faster humans than professionals.

53:12.840 --> 53:21.280
I have achieved statistical significance, but my conclusion is catastrophically wrong because I completely ignored the random variance of the shoe assignment.

53:21.280 --> 53:24.460
That is a phenomenal, perfect analogy.

53:25.140 --> 53:30.100
The statistical test was technically correct about the specific narrow thing it tested.

53:30.740 --> 53:33.580
The amateur in spikes is faster than Bolt and lead shoes.

53:33.580 --> 53:39.540
